# Build output
build/
dist/
*.spec.bak
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Anything carrying a customer's data.
#
# The program is generic; the documents it reads are not. An approved
# manufacturer list is issued to a company under confidentiality, a page
# reading is the content of that company's certificates, and a database is
# every finding about their pipeline. None of it belongs in a public history,
# and a history is not something you can take back.
private/
*.wacache
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
AML*.xlsx
AML*.xlsm
*Approved Material*.xlsx
*AML*.pdf
*Piping AML*
*exceptions.xlsx
*.csv

# Local scratch
.venv/
venv/
.idea/
.vscode/

# Secrets. A public repository is scraped for these within minutes of a push,
# and a database password committed once is a password that must be rotated.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
supabase/.temp/
supabase/.branches/

# Notes and documents dropped in the working folder. One of these held a
# database password and reached a public commit, because a binary carries its
# contents past a scan that reads the text diff.
*.docx
*.doc
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.rtf
*.txt
!requirements.txt
!README.txt

# The approved materials list, bundled into the exe so a machine without one
# beside its jobs still runs the manufacturer-approval checks. It is a
# customer document and this repository is public: the build picks it up from
# here if it is present, and anyone building without it simply gets a program
# that finds the list on disk as before.
weldaudit/data/
